  "summary": "One world, two Runs, and the only thing that differs between them is the class of work each one says it is. The warm Rental holds the whole image and a checked copy of the 40GB dataset and is a second from ready at 4 USD an hour. The cold one holds neither and is fifteen minutes from ready at 2 USD an hour. The batch Run takes the cold machine, because batch work values a second of waiting at a fifth of what the machine doing the waiting costs and the price gap is larger than that. The interactive Run arrives a minute later, by which time the batch Run is holding the cold machine, and takes the warm one and pays double for it: its class prices those thirty-four minutes of waiting at twenty dollars. The decision names the class it was scored for, and the weights it was scored at are in the record beside it. Until the class carried the exchange rate the two terms that convert seconds into dollars were multiplied by zero, so a Run that wanted to start now was placed on whichever machine was a cent cheaper.",
